{"type":"diagram","design":"S66_LAYER_STACK","title":"Quorum and cluster witness design","layers":[{"label":"Quorum witness tier","items":["Third-zone file share witness acts as an independent entity to avoid split-brain states across zones.","Odd vote count enforcement guarantees a definitive majority survives during isolated infrastructure faults.","Tie-breaker during zone loss enables remaining compute nodes to sustain active database workloads."],"icon":"analytics"},{"label":"Primary voting nodes","items":["Zone A primary holds one vote to participate in real-time majority calculations across the active cluster.","Zone B secondary holds one vote, providing local redundancy and sustaining automatic failover decisions.","Dynamic quorum recalculation adjusts total voting weights as nodes disconnect to prevent unplanned outages."],"icon":"security"},{"label":"DR non-voting nodes","items":["Secondary region nodes carry zero votes, ensuring wide-area network drops cannot disrupt primary traffic.","Prevents cross-region split-brain by keeping all quorum arbitration within the primary cloud region.","Zero impact on local quorum ensures remote latency fluctuations never interfere with rapid site failover."],"icon":"automation"},{"label":"Cluster heartbeat fabric","items":["Unicast UDP port 3343 carries heartbeats between cluster nodes to monitor host health continuously.","Relaxed cross-region thresholds prevent transient inter-region packet drops from triggering false alarms.","Last-man-standing protection keeps surviving instances online even after multiple consecutive host drops."],"icon":"team"}],"notes":"To prevent split-brain states during inter-region network degradation, we configure Windows Server Failover Clustering with voting nodes restricted to the primary region and a file share witness in a third zone. The disaster recovery replica in the secondary region is assigned zero quorum votes during normal operations, ensuring WAN instability never takes down the primary clinical cluster.

