{"type":"process","design":"S66_LABEL_ROWS","title":"Zero-downtime OS and SQL patching workflow","lead":"Rolling upgrades patch secondary nodes first and use one controlled failover to maintain cluster availability.","items":[{"label":"Step 1","title":"Patch and validate DR and HA secondaries","points":["Drain read-intent connections from cross-region asynchronous DR replica","Apply Windows Server updates and SQL Server cumulative updates to DR node","Reboot DR replica and verify SYNCHRONIZING state in Availability Group","Patch local synchronous HA secondary node and confirm SYNCHRONIZED state"],"icon":"gear-cloud"},{"label":"Step 2","title":"Execute controlled intra-region failover","points":["Verify zero redo queue depth on patched local synchronous secondary","Issue planned manual failover to promote patched secondary to primary","Internal load balancer health probe shifts traffic to new primary within seconds","Achieve sub-minute cutover with zero transaction loss via synchronous commit"],"icon":"cloud-sync"},{"label":"Step 3","title":"Patch former primary and restore health","points":["Apply identical OS and SQL Server patches to former primary node","Reboot node and rejoin Windows Server Failover Clustering quorum","Confirm all databases reach synchronized state locally and synchronizing in DR","Resume standard read-intent routing across secondary replicas"],"icon":"database-refresh"}],"notes":"Rolling maintenance patches the asynchronous DR replica and local synchronous secondary replica before touching the active primary node. A planned intra-region failover shifts client connections in under one minute with near-zero RPO, keeping the database estate within its availability target during routine patching.

