{"type":"process","design":"S66_LABEL_ROWS","title":"Planned failback and resynchronization runbook","lead":"Returning operations to the primary region uses synchronous resynchronization to protect against data loss.","items":[{"label":"Stage 1","title":"Restore primary site and network health","points":["Verify Dedicated Interconnect and HA VPN links are healthy across regions","Confirm original primary Compute Engine VMs and WSFC nodes are online","Validate Cloud Witness quorum votes and Active Directory replication health"],"icon":"cloud-network-up"},{"label":"Stage 2","title":"Reverse replication to original primary","points":["Rejoin original primary replicas to the Always On Availability Group as secondaries","Stream transaction logs asynchronously from active DR primary back to original region","Monitor log send and redo queues until cross-region catch-up completes"],"icon":"cloud-refresh"},{"label":"Stage 3","title":"Switch mode to synchronous commit","points":["Change availability mode between active DR primary and original primary to synchronous","Wait until database state transitions from synchronizing to synchronized","Confirm zero log send queue and zero redo queue across the inter-region link"],"icon":"database"},{"label":"Stage 4","title":"Execute planned manual failover","points":["Initiate planned manual failover to original primary during a maintenance window","Switch cross-region DR replica back to asynchronous commit to remove commit latency","Re-enable intra-region synchronous automatic failover between primary zones"],"icon":"power-refresh"}],"notes":"After an unplanned regional failover, returning workloads to the primary region requires structured data resynchronization so no transactions committed in the DR region are lost. Temporarily switching the inter-region link to synchronous commit right before the planned failback ensures a zero-data-loss cutover before restoring normal asynchronous DR replication.

